  "mechanisms": [
    "The geometry of perspective dictates that a physical surface not perpendicular to the line of sight projects a distribution of texture-elements that gets denser as the surface recedes, with the gradient of density proportional to the angle of slant.",
    "The retinal image is a geometrical inversion of the visual field, which is defined as a projection of the environment on a 'picture-plane.'",
    "Conflicting cues in the experimental setup (e.g., visibility of projection screen texture, absence of differential blur, hole-screen slant) can lead to a compromise in perceived slant, favoring a frontal impression.",
    "Regular texture may afford a more definite gradient of density than irregular texture, leading to clearer impressions of slant."
  ],
  "moderators": [
    "texture regularity"
  ],
  "implementation_implications": [
    "The perspective of texture, as distinguished from the perspective of rectilinear outlines, seems to be a fundamental basis for the impression of a three-dimensional environment, or what has been called space.",
    "The property of the slant of a surface can be assigned a correlate in retinal stimulation, suggesting that other properties of the phenomenal environment might be similarly accounted for."
  ],
  "domains": [
    "A6_Visual_Form",
    "A3_Spatial_Config"
  ],
